  <section id="what" class="sec prose" data-glyph="何">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      <em>Knots of Existence</em> is a long-running, open visualisation of one question: what, physically, is the thing a foam roller finds
      and a slow exhale releases? It grew from Eugene Jhong’s essays{' '}
      <a href="https://ejhong.substack.com/p/knots-of-existence-hypotheses">Knots of Existence Hypotheses</a> (2026) and{' '}
      <a href="https://ejhong.substack.com/p/knots-of-existence-and-vasocomputation">Knots of Existence and Vasocomputation</a>, and sets their
      answer beside the others that medicine and the contemplative traditions offer. It is not medical advice, and nothing here is a
      protocol.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="measured" class="sec" data-glyph="測">
    <h2>What is measured, and what is representative</h2>
    <dl class="kv">
      <dt>Body</dt>
      <dd>
        The MakeHuman base mesh and its age targets (CC0), averaged across its ethnic targets, from infancy to ninety — male by default, with
        a Form control in the atlas settings that blends to female — smoothed by one level of Catmull–Clark subdivision.
      </dd>
      <dt>Perforators</dt>
      <dd>
        The counts come from the literature: on average 374 major perforators, each at least half a millimetre across, in about forty
        source-artery territories (Taylor &amp; Palmer, 1987), and on the order of a hundred thousand small ones at 4–5 mm spacing, estimated
        from the area of the skin. Major perforators are placed more densely where surgeons find them clustered — the rows beside the spine
        and breastbone, around the navel, on the outer thigh, near the joints — and the rest by blue-noise sampling.{' '}
        <strong>Positions are representative, not a map of any one person</strong>: real perforators vary by centimetres between people and
        between sides.
      </dd>
      <dt>Fascia</dt>
      <dd>
        The superficial and deep fascia sit at typical depths for each region — thin over the scalp, face, hands and feet; thickest over the
        abdomen and buttocks. The exploded view opens the gliding plane and the fat by fixed gaps so the perforators crossing them can be
        seen; the depth control returns the layers to their anatomical depths. The deep channels — the walls between muscles, the raphes,
        and the sleeves around vessels and nerves — are drawn along their landmarks.
      </dd>
      <dt>Trees</dt>
      <dd>
        Each perforator joins its nearest larger vessel, and each major perforator its source artery, by the shortest path along the skin.
        Real source vessels run beneath the deep fascia; the trees are drawn near the surface so they can be read.
      </dd>
      <dt>Knots</dt>
      <dd>
        The model illustrates the theories; it is not a measurement. On the perforator view each site carries a vessel tone, a collar gel and
        a nerve state, coupled as that view describes, with time compressed so a release can be watched. With age every perforator becomes
        likelier to hold — almost none in infancy, about a fifth in the thirties, two-thirds by the late fifties, approaching nine in ten —
        beginning where people report knots (neck and shoulders, the edges of the shoulder blades, the low back and hips, the jaw and belly)
        and spreading along the vessel trees. A young knot is small and changeable; an old one is larger and persists. Small knots are drawn
        as a warm tint on their perforator, medium and major ones as embers. It is a prediction: a census no one has yet taken. The vascular
        latch follows the same curve wherever smooth muscle wraps a tube — the small arteries of the skin and muscle, the great arteries, and the
        walls of the airways, gut and bladder (the organs themselves are not drawn). So do the other theories’ knots: trigger points in the
        taut bands of the muscles Travell and Simons mapped, densified patches in the gliding plane, sensitised nerves where they pierce the
        fascia, and — for perception — places felt on the skin that come and go, with nothing beneath them.
      </dd>
      <dt>Release</dt>
      <dd>
        In the atlas a double-click, or holding the pointer still, presses where it points; a single click selects. On the perforator
        view small knots let go at once and larger ones take more pressure, and what a released knot held passes at once, a little
        weaker, to a nearby perforator of its size — so knots move, stay local, thin out as an area is worked, and grow fewer over time,
        as people describe. A release never sets off another. In the other views a press does what each theory predicts: trigger points,
        patches, nerves and latches let go where they are, and a percept moves elsewhere at once. It is a model of those reports, not a
        measurement. Every theory’s knots are drawn the same way, in the same
        colour; only their place differs.
      </dd>
      <dt>Maps</dt>
      <dd>
        The fourteen channels of Chinese medicine and their 361 points are drawn from the WHO Western Pacific standard (2008): each point is
        placed by its own definition — proportional inches (cun) along a limb, a level and a distance from the midline on the trunk, a
        landmark on the head — on this figure, and each channel runs point to point along the skin. Names follow the standard numbering.
        The sinew channels follow the Ling Shu (chapter 13), with the places each binds (結), their courses simplified to the main lines; the
        trigger-point map marks the usual regions of 45 muscles, after Travell and Simons. All are approximate, for comparison, and not for
        needling. Other traditions’ maps will follow.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="moderation" class="sec prose" data-glyph="中">
    <h2>Moderation</h2>
    <p>
      Go gently. Deep somatic work and intensive meditation carry real dangers, and people have been harmed by doing too much, too fast —
      forceful breathing and breath-holds, hard pressure held too long, long retreats with little sleep. Difficulties can last months or
      years. Never press hard into the neck, where the arteries to the brain lie close beneath the surface. Nothing here is a protocol:
      work in moderation, rest often, and stop when something feels wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are struggling, you are not alone. The{' '}
      <a href="https://sites.brown.edu/britton/research/the-varieties-of-contemplative-experience/">Varieties of Contemplative Experience</a>{' '}
      project at Brown documents meditation-related difficulties, and <a href="https://www.cheetahhouse.org">Cheetah House</a> supports
      meditators in distress. For anything physical, see a physician.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="open" class="sec prose" data-glyph="開">
    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p>
      The code is MIT-licensed and the writing and data are CC BY 4.0, at{' '}
      <a href="https://github.com/ejhong/knots">github.com/ejhong/knots</a>. Hypotheses, maps and references are plain data files; new maps are
      placed by anatomical landmarks rather than coordinates. Corrections from anatomists, surgeons, clinicians and practitioners of the
      traditions are especially welcome. The look follows <a href="https://ejhong.github.io/om/">The OM Project</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="mono small">Three.js · Astro · MakeHuman (CC0) · system type: Georgia, SF Mono</p>
  </section>
